[[:db/retract 17592186338374 :answer-field/choices 17592186336656] [:db/retract 17592186338374 :answer-field/choices 17592186338001] [:db/retract 17592186338374 :answer-field/choices 17592186338146] [:db/retract 17592186338374 :answer-field/choices 17592186336740] [:db/retract 17592186338374 :answer-field/choices 17592186338031] [:db/retract 17592186268476 :answer-field/choices 17592186267491] [:db/retract 17592186268476 :answer-field/choices 17592186268390] [:db/retract 17592186268476 :answer-field/choices 17592186266679] [:db/retract 17592186268476 :answer-field/choices 17592186266969] [:db/retract 17592186268476 :answer-field/choices 17592186268013] {:db/id 17592186124437 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5cb4c36d-f79b-5525-8cf7-91f3e27f40c2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-5cb4c36d-f79b-5525-8cf7-91f3e27f40c2" :question/id #uuid "5cb4c36d-f79b-5525-8cf7-91f3e27f40c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-64966" :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={2}^{x+y}y(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(0,1,0)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(0,1).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & ={2}^{0+1}(1)(0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4d50699f-3ed7-5626-8b63-c6ce285403be"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4d50699f-3ed7-5626-8b63-c6ce285403be" :answer-field/id #uuid "4d50699f-3ed7-5626-8b63-c6ce285403be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-09b9a14b-262a-515e-bd82-1f704a821349" :answer-field/choices ["answer-336e9f14-d6c6-5f83-897b-4c14eb89e5be" "answer-91000328-2b30-5049-9390-2fee6404bd0b" "answer-e4a190e1-968a-5e68-8061-84d550c81b2e" "answer-2590094e-7f95-52df-bdd8-abb0af831110" "answer-09b9a14b-262a-515e-bd82-1f704a821349"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-336e9f14-d6c6-5f83-897b-4c14eb89e5be" :answer/id #uuid "336e9f14-d6c6-5f83-897b-4c14eb89e5be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91000328-2b30-5049-9390-2fee6404bd0b" :answer/id #uuid "91000328-2b30-5049-9390-2fee6404bd0b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4a190e1-968a-5e68-8061-84d550c81b2e" :answer/id #uuid "e4a190e1-968a-5e68-8061-84d550c81b2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2590094e-7f95-52df-bdd8-abb0af831110" :answer/id #uuid "2590094e-7f95-52df-bdd8-abb0af831110" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09b9a14b-262a-515e-bd82-1f704a821349" :answer/id #uuid "09b9a14b-262a-515e-bd82-1f704a821349" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186338913 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,2,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-1 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186338374 :answer-field/correct "answer-9da8c96e-1efe-59f9-a9dc-682a12d5da97" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9da8c96e-1efe-59f9-a9dc-682a12d5da97" "answer-f37ea42d-7c61-5d99-8c24-718ac347ab47" "answer-a7d9df8e-4012-5e27-bf02-9250e930e1d0" "answer-8020f82d-21fe-5f0d-b435-1b1601809556" "answer-7c5ba22e-dbcf-5fa2-95ae-a8ddaa66f720"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9da8c96e-1efe-59f9-a9dc-682a12d5da97" :answer/id #uuid "9da8c96e-1efe-59f9-a9dc-682a12d5da97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f37ea42d-7c61-5d99-8c24-718ac347ab47" :answer/id #uuid "f37ea42d-7c61-5d99-8c24-718ac347ab47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-5\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7d9df8e-4012-5e27-bf02-9250e930e1d0" :answer/id #uuid "a7d9df8e-4012-5e27-bf02-9250e930e1d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4y-3\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8020f82d-21fe-5f0d-b435-1b1601809556" :answer/id #uuid "8020f82d-21fe-5f0d-b435-1b1601809556" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4y-5\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c5ba22e-dbcf-5fa2-95ae-a8ddaa66f720" :answer/id #uuid "7c5ba22e-dbcf-5fa2-95ae-a8ddaa66f720" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=6y-3\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124439 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d880e565-bd50-5672-98e1-45142968e59d" "question-666541af-0f77-5a3b-95c7-e3f70c0a6b57"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d880e565-bd50-5672-98e1-45142968e59d" :question/id #uuid "d880e565-bd50-5672-98e1-45142968e59d" :question/math-academy-id "q-71226" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=5.$ Given that the point $P(4,5,-2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}{2x-y}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=5.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(4,5).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(4,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(4,5) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}{2(4)-(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(4,5) & ={f}_{x}(4,5)(x-4) \\\\ z-(-2) & =1(x-4) \\\\ z+2 & =x-4 \\\\ z & =x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(4,5,-2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=5 \\\\ z=x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=5,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x-6$ (or $x-z-6=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-k)=i+k$ is also parallel to it." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-472a8778-3d0c-5974-8ff7-bc1d5d8b12f2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-472a8778-3d0c-5974-8ff7-bc1d5d8b12f2" :answer-field/id #uuid "472a8778-3d0c-5974-8ff7-bc1d5d8b12f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0fabdc74-7580-5ecf-957d-3da51c5c3bd8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7733cd29-624f-51f2-ae97-a884afdbb38d" "answer-1291615e-eaf6-5649-a612-304df178a8dc" "answer-e4854410-0525-5e63-82a6-354e8d687ef9" "answer-0fabdc74-7580-5ecf-957d-3da51c5c3bd8" "answer-23478b53-a88f-568a-858a-7b25f93a7066"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7733cd29-624f-51f2-ae97-a884afdbb38d" :answer/id #uuid "7733cd29-624f-51f2-ae97-a884afdbb38d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-i+k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1291615e-eaf6-5649-a612-304df178a8dc" :answer/id #uuid "1291615e-eaf6-5649-a612-304df178a8dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,i+k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4854410-0525-5e63-82a6-354e8d687ef9" :answer/id #uuid "e4854410-0525-5e63-82a6-354e8d687ef9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "i+2\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fabdc74-7580-5ecf-957d-3da51c5c3bd8" :answer/id #uuid "0fabdc74-7580-5ecf-957d-3da51c5c3bd8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "i+k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23478b53-a88f-568a-858a-7b25f93a7066" :answer/id #uuid "23478b53-a88f-568a-858a-7b25f93a7066" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "i-2\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-666541af-0f77-5a3b-95c7-e3f70c0a6b57" :question/id #uuid "666541af-0f77-5a3b-95c7-e3f70c0a6b57" :question/math-academy-id "q-71228" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,0,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)-(x+y)^{3}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)-(1+0)^{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,0) & ={f}_{y}(1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =-1(y) \\\\ z+1 & =-y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-1$ (or $y+z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $-j+k.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9d7802a6-4174-5690-9124-8f551b3466e5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9d7802a6-4174-5690-9124-8f551b3466e5" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d7802a6-4174-5690-9124-8f551b3466e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-348a1560-eed6-553f-a07c-d5dbb0b13eae" :answer-field/choices ["answer-759cdd15-f659-5676-8d91-9b242c2d2b7c" "answer-0c7b7abb-15fc-5e25-af71-ce99a58976f7" "answer-348a1560-eed6-553f-a07c-d5dbb0b13eae" "answer-69c34c73-ac38-5363-8c2d-999f5d258274" "answer-14e7d503-39ad-5577-a641-1da1d8c70d06"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-759cdd15-f659-5676-8d91-9b242c2d2b7c" :answer/id #uuid "759cdd15-f659-5676-8d91-9b242c2d2b7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\,j+k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c7b7abb-15fc-5e25-af71-ce99a58976f7" :answer/id #uuid "0c7b7abb-15fc-5e25-af71-ce99a58976f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-j+2\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-348a1560-eed6-553f-a07c-d5dbb0b13eae" :answer/id #uuid "348a1560-eed6-553f-a07c-d5dbb0b13eae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-j+k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69c34c73-ac38-5363-8c2d-999f5d258274" :answer/id #uuid "69c34c73-ac38-5363-8c2d-999f5d258274" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "j+2\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14e7d503-39ad-5577-a641-1da1d8c70d06" :answer/id #uuid "14e7d503-39ad-5577-a641-1da1d8c70d06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "j+k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268885 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=e.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(e,2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(e,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(e,2) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(e,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =1(y-2) \\\\ z & =y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(e,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268476 :answer-field/correct "answer-77cfa3de-e27b-5fe1-b628-9f80c5d48c58" :answer-field/choices ["answer-43046f4c-8850-5dea-9f71-c5e3ebec2500" "answer-3535f187-54bf-56fb-8443-7dab32a9c79d" "answer-77cfa3de-e27b-5fe1-b628-9f80c5d48c58" "answer-3c2c97aa-3243-5467-ac12-075c53d9654e" "answer-5b8e160b-491c-5c8e-996d-6c8ececaab38"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43046f4c-8850-5dea-9f71-c5e3ebec2500" :answer/id #uuid "43046f4c-8850-5dea-9f71-c5e3ebec2500" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=2y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3535f187-54bf-56fb-8443-7dab32a9c79d" :answer/id #uuid "3535f187-54bf-56fb-8443-7dab32a9c79d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77cfa3de-e27b-5fe1-b628-9f80c5d48c58" :answer/id #uuid "77cfa3de-e27b-5fe1-b628-9f80c5d48c58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c2c97aa-3243-5467-ac12-075c53d9654e" :answer/id #uuid "3c2c97aa-3243-5467-ac12-075c53d9654e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+3\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b8e160b-491c-5c8e-996d-6c8ececaab38" :answer/id #uuid "5b8e160b-491c-5c8e-996d-6c8ececaab38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y-2\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-c480752b-2c0f-5bee-93f8-8afec7c25521" :activity/id #uuid "c480752b-2c0f-5bee-93f8-8afec7c25521" :activity/title "Geometric Interpretations of Partial Derivatives" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050158 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-37de0ec1-dde9-55e5-b218-6452fee1d909" "step-dc115997-e200-5133-98f2-30e70726764f" "step-a385dd67-f4d9-5c4e-9dcb-07bacb547fb1" "step-a445cd29-271b-5b63-aa35-8be905e25a57" "step-0dfd5f35-4d13-5e8b-b12c-5286da4ef943"] :activity/first-step "step-37de0ec1-dde9-55e5-b218-6452fee1d909" :db/